# Implement — etude-5f6: `etude gate` walks the invocation-fallback ladder

## What the implementation taught us

**Two defects my own tests caught before any seat saw them**, both in code I had
just written:

1. **Double-run.** The first exhaustion path re-ran the primary after the loop had
   already run it, so a dead primary was invoked twice per gate. Fixed by keeping
   the last executed candidate's result rather than re-running.

2. **A write the manifest would have rejected.** Attaching the ladder note to a
   SUCCESSFUL fallback violates `runmanifest`'s rule that `failure_note` is
   forbidden on `go`/`block` — validated at write time, so the whole gate attempt
   would have failed to record. A fallback that worked would have produced no
   record at all.

   That constraint forced the better design rather than a workaround: on a usable
   verdict the rung that ran is carried by `Harness.Name` (the durable signal a
   reader actually wants), and the fallthrough is recorded at GATE level in
   `decision.degraded_reason` — which is semantically right, because a gate whose
   seat fell through to a fallback did run degraded.

   This is the same class as the `harness.name` catch the opus seat made at plan
   time. The manifest's validation rules are doing real work on this bead.

## Changes

**`internal/liverun/resolve.go`** — `SeatCandidate` and
`ResolveGateSeatCandidates`. `InHarnessPrefix` is exported so the marker has one
definition. `SeatMeta` is built PER CANDIDATE, because
`requiresSessionEvidence` derives from the harness and the harness changes down
the ladder (`claude-code` → `claude-code-subagent` → `agy`); resolving once would
apply the primary's evidence requirement to a fallback with a different one.

**`internal/liverun/engine.go`** — optional `ResolveSeatCandidates` field. When
nil the engine uses the existing single-runner path, so every current caller and
test is untouched. A seat with no configured fallbacks behaves identically either
way, which is every seat except `opus` today.

**`internal/liverun/gate.go`** — `runSeatLadder` walks the candidates:

- a `go` or `block` STOPS the ladder (a real verdict is not an outage);
- only `failed`/`empty`/`malfunction` moves to the next rung;
- an `in-harness:` rung is skipped, never exec'd, and does NOT terminate the
  walk. etude cannot know whether its caller can spawn a sub-agent, so stopping
  there would decide on the caller's behalf and leave a rung it could have run
  unreached. Both plan-gate seats caught that independently;
- exhausted → the last executed result, with the seat's declared identity so
  `harness.name` is never blank;
- nothing exec-able at all → a runner failure, not an empty result, because
  "produced no output" would imply something was attempted.

Notes are machine-readable rather than prose:
`IN_HARNESS_CANDIDATE_SKIPPED harness=… invoke=…` and
`CANDIDATE_FAILED harness=… invoke=… verdict=…`, one line per rung, so a
supervisor can detect the case without string-matching English.

**`internal/cli/gate.go` and `internal/cli/run.go`** — both wire the resolver
from the same closure. Wiring only one would leave two paths resolving seats
differently, which is the second-seat-path problem this machinery exists to
avoid, and would mean a seat that recovers under `etude gate` still dies under
`etude run` for no reason a reader could infer.

## Tests

Five, in `internal/liverun/gate_once_test.go`:

- primary succeeds → fallback never invoked, primary's harness recorded;
- primary fails → verdict comes from the fallback and `Harness.Name` is the
  FALLBACK's, with the failed rung in `degraded_reason` and NO `failure_note`
  (asserted explicitly, since a note there is what breaks the write);
- an `in-harness:` rung is never executed AND does not stop the walk — the
  exec-able rung after it still supplies the verdict;
- exhausted ladder names every rung, keeps `harness.name` non-empty, and the
  attempt is verified present on the run rather than only in memory;
- an all-in-harness ladder executes nothing and still records.

## What this does NOT fix

It does not make a Claude Code supervisor's gate PASS. The in-harness seat is
still skipped, so synthesis still sees too few usable seats and escalates. What
changes is that an exec-able fallback now gets its turn, and when none works the
record names the candidate etude could not run, in a greppable form. Closing the
loop needs verdict injection — **etude-4ed**.

